            ("Why the cheapest quote often costs more later", "<p>A quote that's dramatically below the ranges above is almost always skipping the step that matters most: diamond grinding the slab instead of acid-etching it. Acid etching is faster and cheaper to perform, but it leaves a weaker bond that commonly fails within 1&ndash;3 years &mdash; at which point the floor has to be ground off and redone anyway, so the true cost ends up higher than paying for proper prep the first time.</p>"),
            ("Financing", "<p>Financing is available through [[FINANCING_PARTNER]] with approved credit, which can make a metallic or commercial system more accessible without paying the full cost upfront. Ask about current plans when you request your estimate.</p>"),
            ("How we build a written estimate", "<p>Every estimate we send breaks the job into the same line items: slab prep (grinding and crack repair), base coat material and labor, decorative flake or metallic application if applicable, and topcoat. We measure the space rather than estimating from a phone description, because garage and driveway square footage varies more than most homeowners expect once you account for aprons, steps, and irregular shapes. You'll see each line item before you commit, not a single bundled number.</p>"),
            ("Cost surprises worth asking about upfront", "<p>Three things commonly change a quote after a homeowner has already budgeted for a lower number elsewhere: hidden cracks or spalling revealed once old paint or sealer is ground off, elevated moisture readings that require an upgraded primer, and control joint or expansion joint work on older slabs. Ask any installer directly whether their quote assumes a best-case slab or includes an allowance for these issues &mdash; we quote a not-to-exceed range specifically so surprises don't turn into a change order mid-job.</p>"),
            ("Comparing quotes from different installers", "<p>When comparing bids, ask exactly how each installer preps the slab. A noticeably cheaper quote is very often skipping diamond grinding in favor of acid etching, or skipping crack repair on a slab that clearly needs it. Two quotes that look similar on price can produce very different floors five years later based on that one difference alone.</p>"),
            ("Getting the most accurate quote for your project", "<p>The fastest way to get an accurate number is to send a few photos of the space along with rough dimensions when you request an estimate. Photos let us spot cracking, existing coatings, or moisture staining before we arrive, which means the written estimate you receive is close to the final price rather than a rough range that changes once we see the slab in person. For larger commercial spaces, a brief walkthrough is worth scheduling since traffic patterns and chemical exposure affect which system makes sense as much as square footage does.</p>"),
